<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Corsican Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1755 CE – 1769 CE · capital Corte</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Pasquale Paoli's independent Corsica, whose written constitution of 1755 made it one of the first modern republics and inspired admirers across Europe.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After decades of rebellion against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Genoa</a>, the Corsican assembly elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pasquale-paoli">Pasquale Paoli</a> as General of the Nation in July 1755. In November he gave the island a constitution establishing a national assembly elected by male heads of family, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Corte</a> as the capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Paoli built a state from the interior, founding a university at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Corte</a> in 1765, a mint, a navy and the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lile-rousse">L'Île-Rousse</a>. Justice was reformed to curb vendettas. James Boswell's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-account-of-corsica">Account of Corsica</a> of 1768 made Paoli famous in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> and among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> writers, but <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Genoa</a> still held the main ports.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1768, by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Genoa</a> ceded its rights over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. French troops defeated the Corsicans at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ponte-novu">Ponte Novu</a> in May 1769, Paoli went into exile in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>, and the island became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/corsican-republic.jpg" alt="Corsican Republic" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: Patricia.fidi · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Corsica.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1755 CE – 1769 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Corte</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Pasquale Paoli</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Corsican and Italian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Corsica</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1762&amp;focus=corsican-republic" aria-label="Corsican Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4902 673 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Corsican Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5222 929L5214 863M5222 929L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5214 863h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5222 929h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5222 929h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Corte. <a href="/atlas?year=1762&amp;focus=corsican-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
